# Converted to the revised ten-section template (plus section 11); content and tables carried from the previous version.
BOOK = 'Malachi'
TESTAMENT = 'Old Testament'
GENRE = 'prophets'
SUBTITLE = 'The Burden to Israel, the Messenger of the Covenant, and the Sun of Righteousness'
SECTIONS = [(1, 'Book Setting & Program Identity', [('p', 'This section introduces you to the book by explaining who wrote it, who received it, and what was happening when it was written. It also shows you which of God’s programs the book belongs to — Israel’s prophetic program or the Body of Christ’s mystery program. Knowing the setting and the program identity helps you read the book in its proper context and understand how its teachings fit into the larger story of Scripture.'), ('p', 'Malachi is “the burden of the word of the LORD to Israel by Malachi” (Mal. 1:1). It reproves the priests, charges Judah with treachery against the covenant of the fathers, calls for the tithes to the storehouse, records a book of remembrance for those who feared the LORD, and closes with the day that burns as an oven, the Sun of righteousness, the law of Moses and Elijah the prophet (Mal. 2:10; 3:10, 16–17; 4:1–2, 4–6).'), ('box', 'Program Identity:', 'This book belongs entirely to the Prophecy Program, where the LORD speaks to Israel, to the priests and to the sons of Jacob about the covenant of the fathers, the law of Moses and the great and dreadful day (Mal. 1:1; 3:6; 4:4–5).')]),
(2, 'Narrative Flow of Malachi', [('p', 'This section gives you a simple walkthrough of how the book unfolds. Instead of diving into details right away, it shows the major movements in the book — how its message begins, how it develops, and how it builds toward its close. Seeing the flow of the book helps you understand the big picture before you study the individual passages.'), ('h3', 'Movement 1 — The LORD’s Love and the Priests’ Dishonour (Ch. 1)'), ('p', 'The LORD speaks to Israel and rebukes the priests who despise His name (Mal. 1:1, 6).'), ('h3', 'Movement 2 — Treachery Against the Covenant (Ch. 2)'), ('p', 'The priest’s lips should keep knowledge, and Judah deals treacherously against his brother (Mal. 2:7, 10, 16).'), ('h3', 'Movement 3 — The Messenger and the Tithes (Ch. 3)'), ('p', 'The LORD changes not, calls for the tithes, and writes a book of remembrance (Mal. 3:2, 6, 10, 16–17).'), ('h3', 'Movement 4 — The Day and Elijah (Ch. 4)'), ('p', 'The day burns as an oven, the Sun of righteousness arises, and Elijah the prophet is sent (Mal. 4:1–2, 4–6).')]),
(3, 'Program Themes in Malachi', [('p', 'This section highlights the major ideas emphasized in the book — the big doctrinal themes that shape how Israel should think and live under the covenants. These themes are not verse-by-verse explanations; they are the book’s main emphases, the repeated ideas God wants Israel to carry forward. Seeing these themes together helps you understand the book’s message before you look at the individual truths in the tables.'), ('ol', ['<b>The Burden to Israel</b> — “The burden of the word of the LORD to Israel by Malachi” (Mal. 1:1).', '<b>The Priest’s Lips</b> — “The priest’s lips should keep knowledge, and they should seek the law at his mouth” (Mal. 2:7).', '<b>The Covenant of the Fathers</b> — “Why do we deal treacherously every man against his brother, by profaning the covenant of our fathers?” (Mal. 2:10).', '<b>The LORD Changes Not</b> — “I am the LORD, I change not; therefore ye sons of Jacob are not consumed” (Mal. 3:6).', '<b>The Book of Remembrance</b> — “A book of remembrance was written before him for them that feared the LORD” (Mal. 3:16).', '<b>The Sun of Righteousness</b> — “Unto you that fear my name shall the Sun of righteousness arise with healing in his wings” (Mal. 4:2).', '<b>Elijah Sent</b> — “I will send you Elijah the prophet before the coming of the great and dreadful day of the LORD” (Mal. 4:5).'])]),
(4, 'Christ in Malachi', [('p', 'This section gathers what the book teaches about Christ — through foreshadowing, typology, and prophetic anticipation. These statements help you see the book’s Christ-centered trajectory before you study the doctrinal truths in detail. By collecting these points together, you get a clear picture of how Malachi points to Christ.'), ('ol', ['<b>The Sun of Righteousness</b> — The Sun of righteousness who arises with healing in his wings (Mal. 4:2) is reported as written.', '<b>The Day Like an Oven</b> — The day that burns as an oven, leaving the proud neither root nor branch (Mal. 4:1), stands within Israel’s prophetic hope.', '<b>Elijah Before the Day</b> — The promise to send Elijah before the great and dreadful day (Mal. 4:5) is reported as written; the Gospels’ references to John and Elijah are read in their own chapters.'])]),
(5, 'Israel, the Law, and the Covenants in Malachi', [('p', 'This section helps you see how Malachi speaks about Israel, the law, and the covenants without mixing programs. Malachi operates within Israel’s prophetic framework. By gathering these points together, this section shows you how to read Malachi in its own setting — as an Israel-program book — while recognizing what it says (and does not say) about the Body of Christ.'), ('p', 'Malachi reinforces Israel’s:'), ('ul', ['Israel addressed by the burden of the word of the LORD (Mal. 1:1)', 'the priests, the law of Moses and the covenant of the fathers (Mal. 2:7, 10; 4:4)', 'the sons of Jacob who are not consumed (Mal. 3:6)', 'the tithes brought into the storehouse (Mal. 3:10)']), ('p', 'All content belongs exclusively to the Prophecy Program.')]),
(6, 'Israel-Program Distinctions', [('p', 'This section highlights the teachings in Malachi that belong uniquely to Israel’s prophetic program — truths rooted in prophecy, covenant promises, national identity, and God’s dealings with Israel. By gathering these together, this section helps you see how Malachi contributes to Israel’s story without borrowing from the Body-of-Christ program.'), ('ul', ['The Burden to Israel — “The burden of the word of the LORD to Israel by Malachi.” (Mal. 1:1).', 'The Priest’s Lips — “the priest’s lips should keep knowledge, and they should seek the law at his mouth.” (Mal. 2:7).', 'The Covenant of the Fathers — “profaning the covenant of our fathers.” (Mal. 2:10).', 'Sons of Jacob — “ye sons of Jacob are not consumed.” (Mal. 3:6).', 'The Tithes — “Bring ye all the tithes into the storehouse.” (Mal. 3:10).', 'The Book of Remembrance — “a book of remembrance was written before him for them that feared the LORD.” (Mal. 3:16).', 'The Day Like an Oven — “the day cometh, that shall burn as an oven.” (Mal. 4:1).', 'The Sun of Righteousness — “unto you that fear my name shall the Sun of righteousness arise with healing in his wings.” (Mal. 4:2).', 'The Law of Moses — “Remember ye the law of Moses my servant, which I commanded unto him in Horeb for all Israel.” (Mal. 4:4).', 'Elijah Sent — “I will send you Elijah the prophet before the coming of the great and dreadful day of the LORD.” (Mal. 4:5).'])]),
(7, 'Truths Table: What Malachi Says', [('p', 'This table gathers the key statements, instructions, and teachings in Malachi and shows which ones belong to Israel’s prophetic program. Each row lists a truth from the book, explains what it says, and identifies its program identity based on Israel’s covenant setting. This helps you see, at a glance, what Malachi teaches directly to Israel and keeps those truths separate from universal principles that apply more broadly.'), ('table', ['Category', 'Truth Revealed in Malachi', 'Program Identity', 'Scripture'], [['The Burden to Israel', '“The burden of the word of the LORD to Israel by Malachi.”', 'Israel / Kingdom / Prophecy Explicit Truth', 'Mal. 1:1'], ['The Priest’s Lips', '“the priest’s lips should keep knowledge, and they should seek the law at his mouth.”', 'Israel / Kingdom / Prophecy Explicit Truth', 'Mal. 2:7'], ['The Covenant of the Fathers', '“profaning the covenant of our fathers.”', 'Israel / Kingdom / Prophecy Explicit Truth', 'Mal. 2:10'], ['Sons of Jacob', '“ye sons of Jacob are not consumed.”', 'Israel / Kingdom / Prophecy Explicit Truth', 'Mal. 3:6'], ['The Tithes', '“Bring ye all the tithes into the storehouse.”', 'Israel / Kingdom / Prophecy Explicit Truth', 'Mal. 3:10'], ['The Book of Remembrance', '“a book of remembrance was written before him for them that feared the LORD.”', 'Israel / Kingdom / Prophecy Explicit Truth', 'Mal. 3:16'], ['The Day Like an Oven', '“the day cometh, that shall burn as an oven.”', 'Israel / Kingdom / Prophecy Explicit Truth', 'Mal. 4:1'], ['The Sun of Righteousness', '“unto you that fear my name shall the Sun of righteousness arise with healing in his wings.”', 'Israel / Kingdom / Prophecy Explicit Truth', 'Mal. 4:2'], ['The Law of Moses', '“Remember ye the law of Moses my servant, which I commanded unto him in Horeb for all Israel.”', 'Israel / Kingdom / Prophecy Explicit Truth', 'Mal. 4:4'], ['Elijah Sent', '“I will send you Elijah the prophet before the coming of the great and dreadful day of the LORD.”', 'Israel / Kingdom / Prophecy Explicit Truth', 'Mal. 4:5']], [20, 34, 26, 20])]),
(8, 'Universal Truths Table (All People, All Time)', [('p', 'This table gathers the truths in Malachi that apply to everyone, not just Israel. These are principles rooted in God’s character — things that hold true across all programs and all ages. By separating these universal truths from Israel’s covenant-specific instructions, this section helps you see what Malachi teaches all believers everywhere and what it teaches Israel in its own setting.'), ('table', ['Universal Truth', 'Description', 'Scripture'], [['One Father, One God', '“Have we not all one father? hath not one God created us?”', 'Mal. 2:10'], ['The LORD Changes Not', '“I am the LORD, I change not.”', 'Mal. 3:6'], ['Justice for the Vulnerable', '“against those that oppress the hireling in his wages, the widow, and the fatherless.”', 'Mal. 3:5']], [28, 48, 24])]),
(9, 'Program-Safe Application Grid', [('p', 'This section shows how to apply the teachings of Malachi without mixing programs. The applications come from Israel’s covenant story and from universal truths that apply to everyone. By keeping the applications tied to what the book actually says — and not borrowing commands from Israel’s covenants for the Body of Christ — this grid helps you respond to Malachi in a way that is faithful to its setting, clear, and safe.'), ('ol', ['<b>Honour the one Father</b> — One God created us (Mal. 2:10).', '<b>Trust the LORD who changes not</b> — He is faithful (Mal. 3:6).', '<b>Do not oppress</b> — The hireling, the widow and the fatherless (Mal. 3:5).'])]),
(10, 'Teaching Outline for Malachi', [('p', 'This outline gives you a teacher-ready structure for presenting the book. It follows the narrative movements and highlights the major themes, making it easy to teach Malachi in classes, small groups, or personal study.'), ('olr', ['The LORD’s Love and the Priests’ Dishonour (Ch. 1) — A burden to Israel. <i>(Mal. 1:1, 6)</i>', 'Treachery Against the Covenant (Ch. 2) — One Father. <i>(Mal. 2:7, 10)</i>', 'The Messenger and the Tithes (Ch. 3) — The LORD changes not. <i>(Mal. 3:5–6, 10, 16–17)</i>', 'The Day and Elijah (Ch. 4) — The Sun of righteousness. <i>(Mal. 4:1–2, 4–6)</i>'])]),
(11, 'Companion Usage and Study Suggestions', [('h3', 'How to Use This Companion in Personal Study'), ('p', 'This Companion is designed to walk alongside you as you read Malachi, helping you see both the prophetic structure of the book and the timeless truths rooted in God’s character. Use it slowly, chapter by chapter, letting the narrative movements, themes, and universal truths deepen your understanding. The Program-Safe Applications will guide your heart toward honour toward God, faithfulness, and fear of His name without confusing Israel’s covenant commands with the Body of Christ’s calling today.'), ('h3', 'How to Use This Companion in Small Groups or Classes'), ('p', 'This Companion provides a clear, structured framework that makes teaching Malachi simple and accessible. Each section gives you ready-to-use material: narrative movements for overview, prophecy-program themes for doctrinal clarity, universal truths for discussion, and a pastoral teaching outline for weekly lessons. Use the tables to help your group distinguish Israel’s covenant identity from universal principles, and use the Program-Safe Application Grid to foster meaningful, Christ-centered conversation.'), ('h3', 'How This Companion Fits Within the Acts Overlap Theology Series'), ('p', 'This Companion is part of a larger series designed to help believers read every book of Scripture without blurring God’s prophetic program for Israel and His mystery program for the Body of Christ. Within the Acts Overlap Theology Series, Malachi stands firmly in Israel’s covenant story, yet it still reveals universal truths that shape Christian character today. This Companion shows you how to honor the book’s prophetic context while drawing out the timeless principles that apply to all believers in every age.')])]
